---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About | ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="Learn about Meredith McGee, MPH — a public health writer, researcher, and evaluator driven by community needs and meaningful impact." />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />

		<!-- Page Heading -->
		<section class="about-heading bg-dark bg-pattern">
			<div class="container">
				<h1>Hi, I'm Meredith</h1>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- Essay Body -->
		<section class="about-essay bg-light">
			<div class="essay-body">
				<p>
					I pay attention to how people move through systems: where they're supported, where they get stuck, and what real change requires. Writing is how I make sense of what I notice and how I translate experience into something others can act on. That's what brought me into public health, and it's the thread that still shapes my work today.
				</p>

				<p>
					I'm guided by the belief that public health should respond to real needs and stay accountable to the people it serves. That belief came from early in my life, shaped by an emphasis on service and responsibility that I grew up with in Rochester, New York. I was drawn to history, technical theater, and track and field, and reading and writing were always central to how I understood the world. Looking back, those instincts laid the groundwork for how I approach public health now.
				</p>

				<p>
					At Lafayette College in Easton, Pennsylvania, I pursued both biology and history. I was drawn equally to understanding how bodies function and how social forces shape people's lives. My coursework spanned genetics and genomics on one side, and late twentieth-century history on the other, with a particular focus on LGBTQ+ history and the history of sexuality. I also competed as a Division I track and field athlete before stepping back for medical reasons. That shift deepened my awareness of how systems, bodies, and support structures intersect, and it pushed me to invest more in community-level work.
				</p>

				<figure class="essay-photo">
					<img src="/meredith-headshot.jpg" alt="Meredith McGee" />
				</figure>

				<p>
					During college, I became especially committed to the structural and community conditions that shape health and belonging. I helped establish OUT Lafayette to create more connection among LGBTQ+ students, and I worked to launch Lavender Lane, the college's first gender-affirming and gender-neutral housing option. One of the projects I'm most proud of was bringing panels of the National AIDS Memorial Quilt to campus for the first time in three decades. These efforts reflected an early understanding of health as something shaped not only by individual behavior, but by relationships, policies, and shared spaces. I graduated with honors, and with a much clearer sense of how community-level change could support individual well-being.
				</p>

				<p>
					That orientation carried me to the Yale School of Public Health, where I completed my Master of Public Health in Social and Behavioral Sciences. At Yale, I worked on multiple research studies focused on understanding and improving LGBTQ+ mental health, including an LGBTQ+ affirmative cognitive behavioral therapy trial. I also conducted evaluation work examining how students experience public health internships, and completed research analyzing cannabis use patterns by sexual identity using multi-state BRFSS data. Across all of it, writing remained central. It was how I translated qualitative experience and quantitative data into findings that could inform practice, policy, and decision-making.
				</p>

				<p>
					Alongside my academic work, I served as a development and funding specialist at Bradbury-Sullivan LGBT Community Center. That role let me stay connected to community-based public health while applying my writing skills differently. I came to see development not simply as fundraising, but as a form of public health practice. Through evidence-based, outcomes-driven proposals, I helped secure more than one million dollars in resources that sustained programs and expanded access to care, education, and support. Writing became a way to connect community needs to the structures that make long-term impact possible.
				</p>

				<p>
					More recently, my work with Parabola Center has brought these threads together through a focus on cannabis policy and public health. I've contributed to projects that examine the social and regulatory consequences of legalization, helped communicate the historical arc of prohibition, and moderated conversations that bring public health and policy communities into dialogue. I'm currently developing a qualitative research study focused on consumer experiences in mature recreational cannabis markets. This work reflects a core concern: ensuring that cannabis policy is shaped by evidence, lived experience, and a commitment to health and safety rather than profit alone.
				</p>

				<figure class="essay-photo">
					<img src="/family-photo.jpg" alt="Meredith with K and Ember" />
				</figure>

				<p>
					Outside of work, I'm still drawn to many of the things that first shaped how I see the world. I love theater and storytelling in all their forms. I'm always reading and learning across disciplines, following curiosity wherever it leads. Spending time with my partner, K, and our dog, Ember, is where I feel most grounded. When we can, we get outside and explore nature, slowing down and paying attention in a different way. Lately I've been making more space for painting, as a way to stay curious, present, and connected.
				</p>

				<p>
					What keeps me going is the work that's still unfolding. I'm drawn to questions about how systems function in practice, whose experiences get centered, and how writing can help close the gap between need and response.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	/* Page Heading Section */
	.about-heading {
		padding: 5rem 0 3rem;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.about-heading .container {
		max-width: 800px;
		padding: 0 2rem;
	}

	.about-heading h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.75rem);
		margin: 0;
		line-height: 1.1;
	}

	/* Essay Section */
	.about-essay {
		padding: 4rem 0;
	}

	/* Essay Body Container */
	.essay-body {
		max-width: 800px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 0 2rem;
	}

	.essay-body p {
		margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
		font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1.1vw, 1.05rem);
		line-height: 1.8;
	}

	.essay-body p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	/* Inline Essay Photos */
	.essay-photo {
		max-width: 350px;
		margin: 2.5rem auto;
		border-radius: 12px;
		overflow: hidden;
		box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
	}

	.essay-photo img {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
	}

	/* Responsive */
	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.about-heading {
			padding: 4rem 0 2.5rem;
		}

		.essay-body {
			padding: 0 1.5rem;
		}

		.essay-photo {
			max-width: 280px;
		}
	}
</style>
